# The stack's own schedule (ADR-0036). supercronic runs this file as the
# `ops` service's process, and every line is a one-shot sibling container
# from the same image — `docker compose run` — never a job in the
# scheduler's own process: the deploy's `compose up` recreates the
# scheduler while the deploy runs, and a sibling survives that. Times are
# UTC. The working directory is the instance directory (the service's
# `working_dir`), so `docker compose` here reads the same compose.yml,
# .env and override the host does; -T because there is no terminal, and
# --no-deps because the stack is already up.
#
# Continuous delivery is `deploy --auto`, hourly: it pulls main-latest,
# does nothing when that build is live, defers while an invocation is in
# flight, and rolls back by itself when a build does not come up. Hourly
# rather than per-merge because the fleet merges its own PRs; with the
# idle check, a merge lands on the next quiet hour. Every job exits 0
# quietly when there is nothing to do, so a line in `docker compose logs
# ops` means something happened, and anything that needs a human goes
# through notify (FQ_NOTIFY_HOOK in .env).
17   *  * * *   docker compose run --rm -T --no-deps ops deploy --auto
*/30 *  * * *   docker compose run --rm -T --no-deps ops hygiene
30   3  * * *   docker compose run --rm -T --no-deps ops backup --auto
